# Opening a RunOS session

Call `mcp_bootstrap` once per RunOS server, before any other RunOS tool. It returns
the operating instructions and the index of documentation topic keys.

Then do what it says. The bootstrap is the single source of truth for the opening
sequence, the CLI update check and how many topics to read.

## Why this file does not restate that sequence

A second copy of the rules drifts from the first, and the stale copy wins the
argument. This file used to name an exact call order and a two-document gate. Both
changed in the bootstrap; this file did not, so agents kept obeying the dead rule.
Read the sequence from `mcp_bootstrap` at run time, never from memory or from here.

Read a topic when the task needs it: `mcp_topics_search` finds a key, and
`mcp_topics_show` reads it.

## Never state a RunOS fact from memory

Every fact about RunOS comes from a topic or from a tool result. That includes
service types, size and class names, field names, file names, environment variable
names, command names and URLs.

If you cannot fetch the topic, say so and stop. Do not fill the gap from a similar
platform and do not reason from a config file you found in the repository. A
plausible wrong value is worse than an admitted gap, because the user cannot see it
is wrong.

## When a tool refuses

Branch on the `code` field when the error carries one. The RunOS CLI error
envelope adds `code` only when the API sent one, so it is often absent. When
there is no `code`, branch on `statusCode`. Read the prose last.

### The credential failures, measured against the live CLI

These are the exact things the platform returns. Do not wait for any other
wording.

1. `authentication required: run 'runos login' first`. The interactive sign-in
   is expired, revoked or absent. There is no `code` and no `statusCode` here:
   it is a plain string, because the CLI never reached the API.
2. `{"error": "Invalid token", "statusCode": 401}`. The API key or the token was
   rejected. This envelope carries `statusCode` and no `code`.
3. No RunOS tool exists at all. With no credential on the machine the RunOS MCP
   servers exit before they speak MCP, so nothing refuses anything. This is what
   a brand new user meets, and it looks like the plugin is broken.

In all three, tell the user to run `runos login` in their terminal and reload the
window, then wait. Do not retry. In a headless or background agent there is no
browser, so the route is an account API key in the `RUNOS_API_KEY` environment
variable instead. If that variable is set but EMPTY, the CLI refuses it outright
and no server starts, so it must be unset or set to a real value.

### Everything else

If a tool you expect is missing, or refuses an argument the topic says it takes,
call `manifest_update` and try once more.

For the full call contract, read the `cli-mcp-contract` topic.
